<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Crisis</p><h1 class="enc-title">Munich Agreement</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1938</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Munich Pact</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> Agreement was concluded on 29–30 September 1938 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, without <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> taking part. It required Czechoslovakia to cede the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudetenland">Sudetenland</a> to Nazi Germany, which Hitler had threatened to seize by force. Hailed by Neville Chamberlain as 'peace for our time', it became the symbol of appeasement; Germany occupied the rest of the Czech lands in March 1939.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/munich-agreement.jpg" alt="Munich Agreement" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Munich_Agreement_Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-R69173.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
